          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "With reference to voltage-gated sodium ion channels, explain the difference in speed of transmission of an action potential along a myelinated neurone and a non-myelinated neurone.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 21
            }
          }
        ],
        "topics": [
          {
            "ref": "15.1(7)",
            "role": "primary"
          }
        ],
        "markScheme": {
          "instruction": null,
          "guidance": [],
          "points": [
            {
              "n": 1,
              "text": "myelinated has faster speed (of transmission of action potential) <b>; </b>"
            },
            {
              "n": 2,
              "text": "<i>plus </i><br><i>any </i><b><i>three</i></b><i> from: </i><br>(myelinated) Na<sup>+</sup> channels <b>only</b> occur at nodes of Ranvier<br><b>or</b> (non-myelinated) Na<sup>+</sup> channels occur along length of neurone <b>;</b>"
            },
            {
              "n": 3,
              "text": "(myelinated) depolarisation <b>only</b> occurs at nodes<br><b>or</b> (non-myelinated) depolarisation occurs along length of neurone <b>; </b>"
            },
            {
              "n": 4,
              "text": "(myelinated) long local circuits<br><b>or</b> (non-myelinated) short local circuits <b>; </b>"
            },
            {
              "n": 5,
              "text": "(myelinated) saltatory conduction / described <b>;</b>"
            },
            {
              "n": 6,
              "text": "AVP<b> ;</b> e.g. 100 ms<sup>–1</sup> v 2 ms<sup>–1</sup>"
            }
          ],
          "source": {
            "ms": 21
          },
          "marks": 4
        },
        "er": [
          "er.q9.b"
        ],
        "aiSupplement": {
          "explanation": [
            {
              "text": "The impulse is regenerated where sufficient voltage-gated channels can participate in a local depolarisation. Myelin lets current spread farther between those sites, reducing the number of membrane regions that must undergo sequential action potentials.",
