      "blocks": [
        {
          "heading": null,
          "bullets": [
            "Most candidates were able to name <b>A </b>as the axon and some could identify <b>B </b>as the nucleus of the Schwann cell."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "er.q9.b",
      "scope": "part",
      "target": "q9/b",
      "source": {
        "er": {
          "pdfPages": [
            51
          ],
          "printedPage": null
        }
      },
      "heading": "Question 9 (b)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "heading": null,
          "bullets": [
            "Strong responses stated that myelinated neurones had a faster speed of transmission due to long local circuits and saltatory conduction. These went on to explain that this was due to sodium ion channels only being effective at the nodes of Ranvier where there would be depolarisation of the membrane. A minority of candidates recognised that in a non-myelinated neurone, the depolarisation occurs along the whole length of the neurone because the sodium ion channels are present along the whole length of the neurone, which would mean local circuits would be short."
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
